+### The layer-kernel premise, measured (scripts/inkling-launch-costs.cu on the 3090)
+| cost | us |
+|---|---|
+| kernel launch inside a replayed CUDA graph (82 x 1024 threads) | 1.30 (empty 1-block kernel: 0.73; outside a graph: 2.03) |
+| cooperative launch, same kernel | 1.30 (no extra cost) |
+| cooperative-groups grid barrier | 1.02 |
+| hand-rolled atomic barrier (nanosleep spin) | 2.05 |
+| resident 1024-thread blocks per SM | 1 |
+
+Merging two phases saves a launch (1.3 us) and costs a barrier (1.0 us): ~0.3 us per boundary, so a whole-layer
+kernel of ~40 launches and ~12 barriers would save ~15 us per layer application, ~0.7 ms per token (+7%) at best, not
+the +35-45% estimated in iteration 8 (which assumed ~1.9 us per launch and free barriers). The measured glue2 case adds
+the other cost: one cooperative grid distributing 48 + 216 blocks' work itself took 25 us where two hardware-scheduled
+launches took 20 us. Phase 1 as planned (glue + 5 projections + sinks/taps in one launch: -4-5 launches, +2-3 barriers,
+plus the distribution penalty) nets about zero per layer application and was not built.
+
+Where the 10.5 ms/token go with glue on: ~2080 launches x 1.3 us = 2.7 ms of launch cost; ~7.8 ms of kernel time, of
+which 3.3 ms is weight traffic at peak bandwidth, the MoE products run at 80% of it, the projections at 56-66%, and
+~1400 small kernels take 1.5-2.5 us each (a latency floor independent of the launch). Options on the table: hardware-
+scheduled fusions without barriers on the remaining chains of a layer application (~15-20 launches less per
+application, ~+10%, incremental and bit-exact by body replication); the deep-context attention switches
+(`LLAMA_INKLING_FA_VEC_GQA`, `LLAMA_INKLING_NO_MASK`: the bench loses 20% from short to 54K context and neither was
+ever benchmarked); the layer kernel for the architecture's sake (~+7% at the end).
